{"book":45,"chapter":1,"context":{"chapter":1,"title":"The Gospel and a Wayward World","summary":"Paul introduces himself and his gospel, the power of God for salvation, then describes how humanity traded the truth about God for idols and spiraled into disorder.","background":"Letters in the Roman world opened with the sender, the recipients and a greeting; Paul stretches this opening into a compact summary of the gospel (vv. 1–7). He writes to a church he did not found and had not yet visited (vv. 10–13). Rome was the capital of an empire full of temples and images, so his description of people worshiping images of humans and animals (v. 23) described everyday religious life.","customs":[{"term":"Greeks and barbarians","verses":"1:14","note":"Greek speakers called anyone who did not speak Greek a 'barbarian', mimicking foreign speech as meaningless sounds. Paul uses the pairing to mean everyone, educated or not."}],"watchFor":["Verses 16–17 work as the thesis statement for the whole letter.","The phrase 'God gave them up' or 'over' appears three times (vv. 24, 26, 28)."],"connections":["Habakkuk 2:4","Psalms 19:1","Psalms 106:20","Jeremiah 2:11"]},"verses":{"1":{"w":[["Παῦλος","Paulos","G3972","N-NSM-P","Paul"],["δοῦλος","doulos","G1401","N-NSM","a servant"],["Χριστοῦ","Christou","G5547","N-GSM-T","of Christ"],["Ἰησοῦ","Iēsou","G2424","N-GSM-P","Jesus"],["κλητὸς","klētos","G2822","A-NSM","a called"],["ἀπόστολος","apostolos","G652","N-NSM","apostle"],["ἀφωρισμένος","aphōrismenos","G873","V-RPP-NSM","set apart"],["εἰς","eis","G1519","PREP","for"],["εὐαγγέλιον","euangelion","G2098","N-ASN","[the] gospel"],["θεοῦ","theou","G2316","N-GSM-T","of God"]],"x":[[44009015,44009015,33],[45015016,45015016,24],[46001001,46001001,21],[24001005,24001005,18],[51001001,51001001,17],[56001001,56001001,16],[45001009,45001009,11],[47001001,47001001,11],[46009001,46009001,10],[54001001,54001001,8]],"xn":40},"2":{"w":[["ὃ","ho","G3739","R-ASN","which"],["προεπηγγείλατο","proepēngeilato",